1
00:00:10,947 --> 00:00:12,247
MARK: Petey, are you okay?

2
00:00:17,871 --> 00:00:19,121
Petey?

3
00:00:19,205 --> 00:00:25,127
Yeah, I'm good. I just slipped.
You should invest in a bath mat, maybe.

4
00:00:25,211 --> 00:00:29,757
Yeah, okay. Uh, the robe is
a gift from my brother-in-law.

5
00:00:29,841 --> 00:00:32,927
I've never worn it or anything.
Sorry, it's weird.

6
00:00:34,095 --> 00:00:36,945
Uh… [STAMMERS] …I have pillows.
Do you need anything else?

7
00:00:37,641 --> 00:00:40,691
PETEY: Yeah, I want you to
stop shitting on this awesome robe.

8
00:00:41,895 --> 00:00:43,396
Well, you can keep it.

9
00:00:43,480 --> 00:00:44,772
I will.

10
00:00:44,856 --> 00:00:49,443
Hey. Are you sure nobody can
see in any of these transoms?

11
00:00:49,527 --> 00:00:52,364
Yeah.
Oh, and everything's locked upstairs.

12
00:00:53,782 --> 00:00:56,033
Anybody live in that unit next door?

13
00:00:56,117 --> 00:00:58,995
No, it's just Mrs. Selvig
in the other direction.

14
00:00:59,079 --> 00:01:04,291
Uh, the neighborhood never
really filled up, so it's nice. Quiet.

15
00:01:06,169 --> 00:01:07,963
Fortress of solitude.

16
00:01:08,755 --> 00:01:11,841
I still think an occasional
mixer wouldn't hurt anything.

17
00:01:11,925 --> 00:01:15,302
I checked, and it's not expressly
prohibited in the manual.

18
00:01:15,386 --> 00:01:16,386
Yeah, I bet!

19
00:01:16,470 --> 00:01:19,639
Dude, I pressed Cobel for
an all-floor mixer last quarter.

20
00:01:19,723 --> 00:01:22,517
She said you'll get one when
you earn it, whatever that means.

21
00:01:22,601 --> 00:01:25,145
-Cobel?
-I can't imagine Dylan's 4%

22
00:01:25,229 --> 00:01:26,981
on Sunset Park's gonna help with…

23
00:01:28,607 --> 00:01:29,900
Petey?

24
00:01:31,318 --> 00:01:32,486
You okay?

25
00:01:36,073 --> 00:01:37,825
I'm sorry. Yeah.

26
00:01:39,827 --> 00:01:43,998
With this sickness, I just…
I get disoriented.

27
00:01:44,957 --> 00:01:45,958
It's temporary.

28
00:01:47,960 --> 00:01:49,336
What's Sunset Park?

29
00:01:52,506 --> 00:01:53,549
I don't know.

30
00:04:06,848 --> 00:04:08,558
[SIGHS]

31
00:04:13,229 --> 00:04:16,565
It's like having two different
lives suddenly stitched together.

32
00:04:17,733 --> 00:04:19,652
But the relativity's fucked.

33
00:04:19,736 --> 00:04:23,739
So, my first day at Lumon's
as far back as my fifth birthday.

34
00:04:23,823 --> 00:04:26,909
And with two pasts, it
blurs the present too.

35
00:04:28,328 --> 00:04:30,038
But they said it will get better.

36
00:04:33,249 --> 00:04:34,250
Who's "they"?

37
00:04:37,712 --> 00:04:41,131
"They" is a group of people that
know severance is a blight on mankind.

38
00:04:41,215 --> 00:04:43,215
And they're gonna do something about it.

39
00:04:45,386 --> 00:04:47,263
The Whole Mind Collective?

40
00:04:47,930 --> 00:04:51,684
The kids trolling for signatures downtown?
No, not the fucking WMC.

41
00:04:51,768 --> 00:04:52,602
MARK: Oh.

42
00:04:53,102 --> 00:04:54,103
Someone else.

43
00:04:55,188 --> 00:04:56,481
All right. Well…

44
00:04:59,359 --> 00:05:03,071
So, what do you want from me?

45
00:05:05,823 --> 00:05:08,273
Don't you wanna know
what you're doing down there?

46
00:05:11,537 --> 00:05:16,250
[SIGHS] Okay, look. Uh…
Uh, you know, I… I'm not gonna unsever.

47
00:05:16,834 --> 00:05:20,046
Well, I don't want you to
unsever because that's not a word.

48
00:05:21,089 --> 00:05:22,798
-Reintegration.
-All right. Well, whatever.

49
00:05:22,882 --> 00:05:26,218
I… You know, it hasn't been a, uh…
a blight for me.

50
00:05:26,302 --> 00:05:27,804
It's… It's helped me.

51
00:05:29,305 --> 00:05:32,433
Okay. What if the cost of that help

52
00:05:34,143 --> 00:05:36,020
is that you're murdering
people eight hours a day

53
00:05:36,104 --> 00:05:37,454
and you don't even know it?

54
00:05:38,856 --> 00:05:39,983
Well, am I?

55
00:05:42,986 --> 00:05:47,948
Look, I… I found a department.
One they don't tell us about.

56
00:05:48,032 --> 00:05:49,701
One where you don't get to leave.

57
00:05:51,369 --> 00:05:53,662
Well, don't none of us get to leave?

58
00:05:53,746 --> 00:05:58,334
No, I mean you really don't get to leave.
As in, they're down there right now.

59
00:05:58,418 --> 00:06:00,545
Well, what? Like, chained up, or…

60
00:06:01,212 --> 00:06:02,421
[PETEY SIGHS]

61
00:06:03,630 --> 00:06:04,923
What?

62
00:06:06,591 --> 00:06:08,802
Shh.

63
00:06:10,053 --> 00:06:11,853
I'm not gonna talk about it in here.

64
00:06:12,973 --> 00:06:17,311
I don't know if the monitors are bugged.
Or if Irving's gonna walk in.

65
00:06:25,944 --> 00:06:27,988
Petey, we're not at Lumon.

66
00:06:34,161 --> 00:06:35,561
Then where the fuck is June?

67
00:06:40,417 --> 00:06:41,584
[MARK STAMMERS]

68
00:06:41,668 --> 00:06:46,465
[CRYING, GROANING]

69
00:06:47,257 --> 00:06:49,134
I'm sorry, Mark. [SIGHS]

70
00:06:53,430 --> 00:06:54,431
[SIGHS]

71
00:06:58,310 --> 00:06:59,311
Who's June?

72
00:07:02,648 --> 00:07:07,569
June is my daughter. Greatest kid
on Earth and a hell of a guitar player.

73
00:07:13,700 --> 00:07:16,119
How happy are you that
you asked me to stay here?

74
00:07:46,650 --> 00:07:48,235
MS. COBEL: Oh, Mark.

75
00:07:50,988 --> 00:07:52,698
Are you all right?

76
00:07:54,950 --> 00:07:59,037
<i>But what do you say, Natalie, to the
woman who became pregnant at work</i>

77
00:07:59,121 --> 00:08:01,456
<i>less than a month after
her company went severed?</i>

78
00:08:01,540 --> 00:08:03,958
NATALIE: <i>Well, first, I'd
suggest she reveal her identity</i>

79
00:08:04,042 --> 00:08:05,668
<i>if she wants an honest conversation</i>

80
00:08:05,752 --> 00:08:07,795
<i>-about her experience.
-How could she have that conversation</i>

81
00:08:07,879 --> 00:08:11,279
<i>-when her workie was the one involved…
-Okay, first of all, workie?</i>

82
00:08:11,425 --> 00:08:13,509
<i>That, as I understand, is
the preferred term among…</i>

83
00:08:13,593 --> 00:08:16,346
<i>I think it's exactly the
condescending verbiage that I'd expect</i>

84
00:08:16,430 --> 00:08:18,973
<i>from a group trying to save
people from their own choices.</i>

85
00:08:19,057 --> 00:08:20,767
WMC SPOKESPERSON: <i>Choices?
Did her insie have a…</i>

86
00:08:20,851 --> 00:08:22,810
NATALIE: <i>Insie?
Where are you getting these terms?</i>

87
00:08:22,894 --> 00:08:25,897
WMC SPOKESPERSON: <i>Fine. Call it someone
who's had their brain split in half…</i>

88
00:08:25,981 --> 00:08:28,148
NATALIE: <i>That has nothing
to do with what we do at Lumon</i>

89
00:08:28,232 --> 00:08:29,733
-<i>nor any severance procedure.</i>
-[SIGHS]

90
00:08:29,817 --> 00:08:31,485
<i>I understand you don't
wanna answer my questions…</i>

91
00:08:31,569 --> 00:08:33,819
<i>-I'm answering your questions.
-…because it'll reveal that you are</i>

92
00:08:33,903 --> 00:08:36,698
-<i>complicit in something totally immoral.</i>
-NATALIE: <i>What's immoral is</i>

93
00:08:36,782 --> 00:08:39,492
-[SIGHING]
-[REMOTE CONTROL CLICKING]

94
00:08:39,576 --> 00:08:42,537
-[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]
-CHARACTER 1: <i>Tell me it's not true.</i>

95
00:08:42,622 --> 00:08:44,540
[LAUGHING CONTINUES]

96
00:08:44,624 --> 00:08:47,126
CHARACTER 2: <i>Well, I want to, but I can't.
See?</i>

97
00:08:48,252 --> 00:08:50,128
REPORTER: <i>…continuing
through the greater basin,</i>

98
00:08:50,212 --> 00:08:54,633
<i>probably into the afternoon, just as
we predicted yesterday.</i> [CHUCKLES]

99
00:08:54,717 --> 00:08:58,345
<i>The good news is, those clouds should
dissipate somewhere over the Ganz area…</i>

100
00:08:58,429 --> 00:08:59,680
[SIGHS]

101
00:08:59,764 --> 00:09:03,100
REPORTER: <i>…leaving a clear, sunny
day, for our friends in and around Kier.</i>

102
00:09:03,184 --> 00:09:05,894
<i>That said, the cold's
not letting up anywhere.</i>

103
00:09:05,978 --> 00:09:08,772
<i>And those roads are going to
remain icy throughout the week</i>

104
00:09:08,856 --> 00:09:10,274
<i>and on into the weekend.</i>

105
00:09:10,358 --> 00:09:13,058
<i>So wherever you're headed,
please do commute with care.</i>

106
00:09:20,326 --> 00:09:21,327
Petey.

107
00:09:24,288 --> 00:09:27,541
Wake up, Petey. Petey?

108
00:09:33,714 --> 00:09:34,715
Hey.

109
00:09:35,424 --> 00:09:37,802
It's morning. You are in my basement.

110
00:09:40,137 --> 00:09:41,347
Right.

111
00:09:42,014 --> 00:09:46,518
So I'm going to work like you said.
Uh, you can stay here if you want.

112
00:09:46,602 --> 00:09:50,230
Um, I just wanna let you know, uh

113
00:09:50,314 --> 00:09:54,151
[STAMMERS] I'm not
gonna reintegrate, you know?

114
00:09:54,944 --> 00:09:58,364
[SCOFFS] Okay.

115
00:09:59,156 --> 00:10:02,159
I lost my wife a couple
years ago in a car accident.

116
00:10:02,868 --> 00:10:06,121
This is, uh… It's helping me, you know?

117
00:10:06,205 --> 00:10:08,582
-I'm sorry, Mark.
-No, no, no. No.

118
00:10:10,876 --> 00:10:11,961
At work…

119
00:10:14,880 --> 00:10:19,093
You'd come in sometimes with red eyes.

120
00:10:20,594 --> 00:10:23,847
We had a joke that you
had an elevator allergy.

121
00:10:23,931 --> 00:10:24,974
[CHUCKLES]

122
00:10:25,683 --> 00:10:27,184
There was even a song for it.

123
00:10:29,520 --> 00:10:30,896
But I always wondered.

124
00:10:32,815 --> 00:10:34,734
You carry the hurt with you.

125
00:10:35,526 --> 00:10:38,946
You feel it down there too.
You just don't know what it is.

126
00:10:42,283 --> 00:10:43,367
Okay.

127
00:10:44,410 --> 00:10:47,329
Why don't you, uh, grab
whatever you want up in the fridge?

128
00:10:47,413 --> 00:10:48,863
I should be back around 6:00.

129
00:11:02,719 --> 00:11:05,639
-[BLOW-DRYER WHIRRING]
-Oh, hi!

130
00:11:06,389 --> 00:11:09,976
-Hey.
-Oh, Mark, I'm sorry for the racket!

131
00:11:10,477 --> 00:11:13,354
I'm just deicing my stoop.

132
00:11:13,438 --> 00:11:17,275
Oh, no worries.
Uh, thanks again for the cookies.

133
00:11:17,359 --> 00:11:18,860
I'll bring by more!

134
00:11:20,195 --> 00:11:21,237
Please.

135
00:11:27,786 --> 00:11:29,537
[CAR ENGINE STARTS]

136
00:11:41,675 --> 00:11:42,676
[WHIRRING STOPS]

137
00:11:55,397 --> 00:11:57,797
-JUDD: Mr. Scout, how are you doing?
-Hey, Judd.

138
00:11:58,358 --> 00:12:00,610
[BUZZING]

139
00:12:07,075 --> 00:12:08,285
[BEEPS]

140
00:12:38,106 --> 00:12:41,692
I did a thing.
I deleted the scary numbers.

141
00:12:41,776 --> 00:12:42,777
When?

142
00:12:42,861 --> 00:12:45,863
-Yesterday. You were gone.
-I was?

143
00:12:45,947 --> 00:12:49,742
Oh, yeah. Dylan took over training
in your stead to great success.

144
00:12:49,826 --> 00:12:53,204
You look trim, Mark.
I wonder if you were food poisoned.

145
00:12:53,288 --> 00:12:55,414
Welcome back, boss. New photos come in?

146
00:12:55,498 --> 00:12:58,293
Oh, good.
You can finally get rid of those old ones.

147
00:13:02,672 --> 00:13:06,272
You may as well skip it actually,
'cause I put in my resignation request.

148
00:13:06,885 --> 00:13:08,678
-You did submit it?
-I did.

149
00:13:10,013 --> 00:13:13,213
It's good. It means you won't get
sent to the break room anymore.

150
00:13:14,559 --> 00:13:16,017
MR. MILCHICK: Hello, Refiners.

151
00:13:16,101 --> 00:13:17,143
Hello, Mr. Milchick.

152
00:13:17,227 --> 00:13:18,937
Mark, I spoke to your outie yesterday.

153
00:13:19,021 --> 00:13:21,064
He sounded just
shattered not to be at work.

154
00:13:21,148 --> 00:13:22,566
But now that you're here,

155
00:13:22,650 --> 00:13:25,850
care to read your first morning
announcements as department head?

156
00:13:26,320 --> 00:13:27,863
Oh! Uh, sure.

157
00:13:29,073 --> 00:13:32,201
Should be easier without me in
the, uh, audience. [CHUCKLES]

158
00:13:34,203 --> 00:13:37,414
[WHISPERING] Mark used to find it
funny to interrupt Petey's announcements.

159
00:13:37,498 --> 00:13:39,083
With gas having.

160
00:13:41,085 --> 00:13:45,965
Okay, quiet down, all.
I'm Mark, your new announcements person.

161
00:13:46,966 --> 00:13:47,967
[SHUTTER CLICKS]

162
00:13:49,551 --> 00:13:51,761
Uh, just a couple things here.

163
00:13:51,845 --> 00:13:54,598
Why are you standing weird? Stand normal.

164
00:13:55,641 --> 00:13:57,518
I'm… I'm standing great.

165
00:13:58,018 --> 00:14:00,604
Are you uncomfortable?
We could all look away?

166
00:14:00,688 --> 00:14:03,940
Okay, there's been some
garbage in the recycling.

167
00:14:04,024 --> 00:14:07,819
-Please, everybody, don't do that. Um…
-[SHUTTER CLICKS]

168
00:14:07,903 --> 00:14:12,908
Oh, as a reminder, Post-it
Notes are not to be put on faces.

169
00:14:12,992 --> 00:14:14,618
They clog your outie's pores.

170
00:14:15,327 --> 00:14:18,914
That one's about me.
I do this beloved character, Sticky Head.

171
00:14:18,998 --> 00:14:23,001
Also, please refrain from boasting
about previously earned waffle parties?

172
00:14:23,085 --> 00:14:24,086
Feeling attacked.

173
00:14:24,795 --> 00:14:26,338
Uh, and lastly…

174
00:14:28,048 --> 00:14:29,883
Oh, um, uh…

175
00:14:31,176 --> 00:14:34,726
Well, I've never seen a resignation
request get such a quick turnaround.

176
00:14:36,515 --> 00:14:39,226
Do I have to finish out the
day or can I skip out now?

177
00:14:44,982 --> 00:14:48,110
Lastly, Helly's resignation request, uh,

178
00:14:49,820 --> 00:14:50,820
was denied.

179
00:14:50,904 --> 00:14:51,905
Hmm.

180
00:14:53,616 --> 00:14:55,116
Yeah, no, that can't be right.

181
00:14:55,951 --> 00:14:57,251
My outie wouldn't do that.

182
00:15:01,874 --> 00:15:03,834
You know, Petey used to say…

183
00:15:12,509 --> 00:15:14,637
[TYPING]

184
00:15:20,726 --> 00:15:24,146
[LAUGHS] Oh, this is so fun.

185
00:15:25,439 --> 00:15:30,151
Shall I, you know, tiptoe
up and sneak to the door?

186
00:15:30,235 --> 00:15:32,404
-He's at work, baby. It doesn't matter.
-How do you know?

187
00:15:32,488 --> 00:15:34,990
-Because his car's not here.
-Maybe he played hooky.

188
00:15:35,074 --> 00:15:36,908
Can you go do it? 'Cause I gotta pee.

189
00:15:36,992 --> 00:15:38,077
Yes. [CLEARS THROAT]

190
00:15:51,422 --> 00:15:54,300
Against the door, or to the side?

191
00:15:54,384 --> 00:15:56,052
I think either's really good.

192
00:15:58,179 --> 00:16:00,139
-Do you think he'll be surprised?
-I do.

193
00:16:00,807 --> 00:16:03,392
My babe, I'm gonna put it off to the side.

194
00:16:03,476 --> 00:16:04,477
Awesome.

195
00:16:09,232 --> 00:16:10,233
[GRUNTS]

196
00:16:14,279 --> 00:16:15,279
-Perfect.
-Good.

197
00:16:15,363 --> 00:16:17,156
-I mean, it's perfect.
-Yes.

198
00:16:22,537 --> 00:16:25,748
[CHUCKLES, SQUEALS]
He's gonna be so excited.

199
00:16:25,832 --> 00:16:27,291
-Yeah.
-I hope he comes home early.

200
00:16:27,375 --> 00:16:28,501
He's not going to.

201
00:17:04,621 --> 00:17:06,955
[SIGHS, INHALES SHARPLY]

202
00:17:23,222 --> 00:17:25,849
[GRUNTS, GROANS]

203
00:17:28,895 --> 00:17:30,855
Hey. What's that? Sudoku?

204
00:17:31,481 --> 00:17:32,482
It's nothing.

205
00:17:33,149 --> 00:17:34,275
What were you drawing?

206
00:17:34,359 --> 00:17:36,986
You getting back to work, in cubist form.

207
00:17:37,070 --> 00:17:40,239
Kay, two things.
How do you know what cubist form is

208
00:17:40,323 --> 00:17:42,673
-[CHUCKLES]
-…and what the fuck are you wearing?

209
00:17:45,703 --> 00:17:46,829
Oh, fuck.

210
00:17:51,042 --> 00:17:52,043
[DOOR CLOSES]

211
00:18:11,687 --> 00:18:13,105
[DOOR OPENS]

212
00:18:55,981 --> 00:18:56,982
No.

213
00:18:59,484 --> 00:19:00,485
[BREATHES SHAKILY]

214
00:19:16,752 --> 00:19:18,086
[CELL PHONE BUZZING]

215
00:19:19,755 --> 00:19:21,965
[SIGHS] Oh.

216
00:19:22,716 --> 00:19:25,177
-Yes?
-[MR. MILCHICK SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

217
00:19:29,598 --> 00:19:32,798
MS. COBEL: [ECHOING] What did
he tell her after the announcement?

218
00:19:34,686 --> 00:19:37,981
[SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

219
00:19:44,279 --> 00:19:45,739
No, it's not so bad.

220
00:19:48,075 --> 00:19:50,077
[SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

221
00:19:52,788 --> 00:19:55,624
Oh, just mildly aggressive.

222
00:19:58,168 --> 00:20:00,128
[NORMAL VOICE] Did
you give her directed praise?

223
00:20:00,212 --> 00:20:02,088
[MR. MILCHICK SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

224
00:20:02,172 --> 00:20:05,322
-Uh-huh. [SIGHING] Uh-huh.
-[MR. MILCHICK CONTINUES, INDISTINCT]

225
00:20:05,592 --> 00:20:06,593
I'm coming.

226
00:20:41,127 --> 00:20:42,128
[BREATHES DEEPLY]

227
00:20:46,549 --> 00:20:48,384
[PANTING]

228
00:20:54,724 --> 00:20:56,100
[GRUNTS]

229
00:20:59,103 --> 00:21:00,271
[GROANS]

230
00:21:08,821 --> 00:21:11,281
MARK: [KNOCKS] <i>Hey, Helly?</i>

231
00:21:11,365 --> 00:21:12,366
HELLY: Go away.

232
00:21:12,867 --> 00:21:14,826
-Are you okay?
-I'm fine.

233
00:21:14,910 --> 00:21:16,710
I mean, it's been, like, 45 minutes.

234
00:21:19,081 --> 00:21:20,082
[SIGHS]

235
00:21:21,083 --> 00:21:23,210
-Uh…
-I'm using the bathroom.

236
00:21:23,878 --> 00:21:27,631
Okay. It's just… It's b…
It-It-It has been a while.

237
00:21:27,715 --> 00:21:30,343
-I'm fine, Mark.
-I… I… I think I need to come in.

238
00:21:31,010 --> 00:21:32,637
-MARK: That's… Okay?
-No!

239
00:21:33,220 --> 00:21:36,265
I am going to come in, so get… get ready.

240
00:21:36,349 --> 00:21:38,392
Do the things you need
to do 'cause I'm comin' in.

241
00:21:38,476 --> 00:21:39,476
No!

242
00:21:39,560 --> 00:21:41,645
-Okay, here I come. I'm sorry.
-Mark, no! I'm not decent!

243
00:21:41,729 --> 00:21:43,279
-Do… Jesus.
-I'm sorry. I'm…

244
00:21:46,484 --> 00:21:48,068
Writing the note directly on your skin.

245
00:21:48,152 --> 00:21:51,905
Uh, well, unfortunately, the detectors
can pick up lettering anywhere.

246
00:21:51,989 --> 00:21:53,991
Does this look like lettering to you?

247
00:21:54,075 --> 00:21:56,035
Well, if you put your arms together.

248
00:21:57,119 --> 00:21:59,956
-[SIGHS]
-Huh. Not bad.

249
00:22:01,999 --> 00:22:04,960
I thought you were starting
to, like, be okay here.

250
00:22:05,044 --> 00:22:06,545
What, 'cause I did the numbers thing?

251
00:22:06,629 --> 00:22:09,798
Well, you got a win. You know,
a lot of people find joy in that.

252
00:22:09,882 --> 00:22:11,758
It literally causes fear.

253
00:22:11,842 --> 00:22:14,469
That's just one subset of numbers.
The others are more comforting…

254
00:22:14,553 --> 00:22:16,263
Mark, I don't wanna work here with you.

255
00:22:16,347 --> 00:22:19,099
So don't burst into the bathroom
with your pathetic boss voice on

256
00:22:19,183 --> 00:22:20,833
and try to convince me that I do.

257
00:22:24,814 --> 00:22:26,857
Okay, I'll give you five
minutes to scrub your arms.

258
00:22:26,941 --> 00:22:28,192
Or what?

259
00:22:28,943 --> 00:22:32,143
[WHISPERS] Do you want…
Do you want Graner to use the bad soap?

260
00:22:33,572 --> 00:22:34,740
There's bad soap?

261
00:22:39,870 --> 00:22:41,080
Thanks, boss.

262
00:22:42,123 --> 00:22:43,823
Yeah, I fucking love working here.

263
00:22:44,292 --> 00:22:45,417
-Mark.
-Irv.

264
00:22:45,501 --> 00:22:47,669
I was listening at the door.
I heard it all.

265
00:22:47,753 --> 00:22:49,755
-Okay.
-I am troubled by you having

266
00:22:49,839 --> 00:22:53,383
high-stress exchanges like that
so fresh off your food poisoning.

267
00:22:53,467 --> 00:22:54,675
It's fine.

268
00:22:54,759 --> 00:22:57,011
The loathing with which she spoke to you.

269
00:22:57,095 --> 00:22:58,554
Well, acclimating takes time.

270
00:22:58,638 --> 00:22:59,889
And guidance.

271
00:23:01,600 --> 00:23:03,000
What's on your mind, Irving?

272
00:23:05,854 --> 00:23:08,898
Dylan was going on all
yesterday about incentives,

273
00:23:08,982 --> 00:23:12,610
pencil erasers and waffle parties,
as though that's why we're here.

274
00:23:12,694 --> 00:23:14,321
That's not why we're here.

275
00:23:15,488 --> 00:23:21,410
If it's a deeper meaning she craves,
she should see the perpetuity wing.

276
00:23:21,494 --> 00:23:23,663
She can read about all
that stuff in the handbook.

277
00:23:23,747 --> 00:23:27,291
You know it's different to be there.
To see it.

278
00:23:27,375 --> 00:23:28,543
[SIGHS]

279
00:23:29,377 --> 00:23:31,796
It just seems premature.
I mean, that place is a lot.

280
00:23:31,880 --> 00:23:36,301
Correct. That place is everything.

281
00:23:41,097 --> 00:23:42,098
How'd the check go?

282
00:23:42,641 --> 00:23:46,269
There was a package left at his door
from someone. Open it, would you?

283
00:23:49,522 --> 00:23:51,983
-MS. COBEL: Oh, God.
-Is this the brother-in-law?

284
00:23:52,067 --> 00:23:53,777
Ricken. His fifth book.

285
00:23:57,280 --> 00:24:01,158
"Chapter 12: On Learning to Be
Emotionally Nude in Front of My Wife."

286
00:24:01,242 --> 00:24:04,162
MS. COBEL: Check it for
messages, just to be safe.

287
00:24:04,746 --> 00:24:05,872
Mark.

288
00:24:07,290 --> 00:24:09,250
-Can I help you?
-Does she have a moment?

289
00:24:09,334 --> 00:24:13,129
Did you fill out an official
request for supervisory interaction?

290
00:24:13,755 --> 00:24:15,298
Uh, [SIGHS] no, I…

291
00:24:15,382 --> 00:24:16,841
MS. COBEL: It's fine. Come in.

292
00:24:16,925 --> 00:24:18,802
Sorry. I can fill out the form.

293
00:24:19,511 --> 00:24:20,611
MS. COBEL: What is it?

294
00:24:22,597 --> 00:24:23,974
-Um…
-Get the door.

295
00:24:27,644 --> 00:24:31,689
Uh, Irving thought we should take
Helly to the perpetuity wing today.

296
00:24:31,773 --> 00:24:34,609
Well, it's a good thing I
made Irving department chief.

297
00:24:34,693 --> 00:24:40,990
Sorry. I think I should take Helly
and the team to the perpetuity wing.

298
00:24:41,074 --> 00:24:44,535
And have you filled out a
common-space reservation slip?

299
00:24:44,619 --> 00:24:48,915
-[SIGHS] No, I…
-Mark.

300
00:24:48,999 --> 00:24:52,376
I'm sorry. Uh, I…
I've just been… I don't know.

301
00:24:52,460 --> 00:24:55,130
Uh, it's been weird since Petey left.

302
00:24:56,756 --> 00:25:00,844
I mean, you know, he…
he was… He set the tone.

303
00:25:02,929 --> 00:25:07,017
If Petey were the tone-setter
you're lionizing him as,

304
00:25:07,892 --> 00:25:09,519
he'd still be here.

305
00:25:11,479 --> 00:25:13,148
Wh… What does that mean?

306
00:25:14,941 --> 00:25:17,611
Are you gonna make me throw my mug at you?

307
00:25:19,153 --> 00:25:20,904
I… [STAMMERS] Why would you…

308
00:25:22,990 --> 00:25:25,534
-Get MDR to its numbers.
-Okay.

309
00:25:28,704 --> 00:25:29,705
Mark?

310
00:25:31,332 --> 00:25:35,961
What I just did was something I knew
that you could handle and grow from.

311
00:25:37,129 --> 00:25:39,465
It was very painful for me.

312
00:25:41,425 --> 00:25:43,552
I hope that you'll let it help you.

313
00:25:50,017 --> 00:25:52,353
Um, open or closed?

314
00:25:58,275 --> 00:25:59,443
Both.

315
00:26:03,697 --> 00:26:05,074
I'll just… [GRUNTS]

316
00:26:09,995 --> 00:26:15,041
My delts are embarrassingly good today.
My outie does muscle shows for sure.

317
00:26:15,125 --> 00:26:18,003
If your outie did muscle shows,
you wouldn't have to work here.

318
00:26:18,087 --> 00:26:19,963
I'm sorry, do you know how
much muscle shows pay?

319
00:26:20,047 --> 00:26:21,548
No, none of us can know that.

320
00:26:21,632 --> 00:26:23,425
IRVING: I imagine it's a tiered system.

321
00:26:23,509 --> 00:26:27,345
Perhaps there are monetary
prizes for gold, silver and bronze.

322
00:26:27,429 --> 00:26:31,600
DYLAN: No, uh, my guess is that
they do it piecemeal per muscle. So…

323
00:26:31,684 --> 00:26:34,060
-IRVING: No, I don't think so.
-DYLAN: Best delt, 30 bucks.

324
00:26:34,144 --> 00:26:38,231
Best ab, 20 bucks.
Biceps are a little flashier, 75 bucks.

325
00:26:38,315 --> 00:26:39,983
I would think lats carry a high value.

326
00:26:40,067 --> 00:26:42,819
They're considered very
attractive in muscle-building circles.

327
00:26:42,903 --> 00:26:44,696
And in society at large.

328
00:26:44,780 --> 00:26:46,281
DYLAN: Lats are bullshit, dude…

329
00:26:46,365 --> 00:26:49,451
-IRVING: The center of a good physique.
-DYLAN: You are alone on this one, Irv…

330
00:26:49,535 --> 00:26:51,536
-You've never noticed my lats?
-…I assure you. Never once.

331
00:26:51,620 --> 00:26:56,750
IRVING: You don't understand why my
posture's ramrod straight. You see how…

332
00:27:04,091 --> 00:27:06,051
-[INHALES SHARPLY]
-MARK: Thirsty?

333
00:27:07,344 --> 00:27:08,345
Mm-hmm.

334
00:27:09,305 --> 00:27:12,557
Hey, uh, just in case you find
this relevant for some reason,

335
00:27:12,641 --> 00:27:17,438
the code detectors can read
messages hidden inside the body as well.

336
00:27:20,274 --> 00:27:26,071
Also, when that happens, it's Milchick's
job to extract the message from you.

337
00:27:26,655 --> 00:27:31,326
And when he asks how
long ago you ingested it,

338
00:27:31,410 --> 00:27:34,705
I really can't recommend honesty enough.

339
00:27:36,874 --> 00:27:40,169
It's easier for you both if he
knows which end to start from.

340
00:27:50,345 --> 00:27:51,638
Another good try though.

341
00:27:53,514 --> 00:27:54,515
Mm-hmm.

342
00:27:56,643 --> 00:27:58,936
Go shut down your workstation.
We're taking a trip.

343
00:27:59,020 --> 00:28:00,563
DYLAN: The power is in your calves.

344
00:28:00,647 --> 00:28:04,647
IRVING: …and it's always been that way.
I can tell you that right now, young boy.

345
00:28:05,360 --> 00:28:07,820
All eight CEOs have
been of the Eagan lineage.

346
00:28:07,904 --> 00:28:09,780
A line dating back to the founder.

347
00:28:09,864 --> 00:28:12,950
I have a mnemonic poem to
help you remember their names.

348
00:28:13,034 --> 00:28:16,453
I authored it myself, and of course,
it falls outside corporate canon,

349
00:28:16,537 --> 00:28:18,831
and it's poetically imperfect, to be sure.

350
00:28:18,915 --> 00:28:22,918
There's a slant rhyme in the second
verse, of "Ambrose" to "ban those,"

351
00:28:23,002 --> 00:28:26,755
which was necessary to further
the narrative, but regrettable.

352
00:28:26,839 --> 00:28:30,593
Ambrose is sometimes unfairly maligned as
a black sheep in the annals of the family.

353
00:28:30,677 --> 00:28:31,677
What's this?

354
00:28:31,761 --> 00:28:34,680
IRVING: And it certainly wasn't
my intent to slight him further,

355
00:28:34,764 --> 00:28:37,164
-but I think you'll find…
-HELLY: "Eagan bingo"?

356
00:28:37,350 --> 00:28:38,767
Hey. Eagan bingo.

357
00:28:38,851 --> 00:28:42,354
-Oh. W-We shouldn't, right? This…
-IRVING: …for when we arrive…

358
00:28:42,438 --> 00:28:44,440
-Hey, what the hell is Eagan bingo?
-[SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

359
00:28:44,524 --> 00:28:46,901
It's how you don't die of
boredom in the perpetuity wing.

360
00:28:46,985 --> 00:28:48,986
And come on, it'll be
an excellent opportunity

361
00:28:49,070 --> 00:28:50,780
for everyone except Irv to bond.

362
00:29:02,375 --> 00:29:03,626
Optics and Design.

363
00:29:05,044 --> 00:29:06,796
Macrodata Refinement.

364
00:29:08,715 --> 00:29:09,715
Burt.

365
00:29:09,799 --> 00:29:10,800
Hi.

366
00:29:11,509 --> 00:29:13,302
I trust that your session was good.

367
00:29:13,386 --> 00:29:15,095
Great, very restorative.

368
00:29:15,179 --> 00:29:16,639
Wa… You two know each other?

369
00:29:16,723 --> 00:29:21,685
We were just admiring some
Lumon artwork together, very briefly.

370
00:29:21,769 --> 00:29:24,522
Remember, we'll have those
new handbook totes soon.

371
00:29:24,606 --> 00:29:25,731
So exciting.

372
00:29:25,815 --> 00:29:28,025
Thanks for the tip.
You got business, or you out for a stroll?

373
00:29:28,109 --> 00:29:30,945
-Dylan.
-DYLAN: What? I think you guys are cool,

374
00:29:31,029 --> 00:29:34,448
and I'm just wondering what you're up to.
It's rare seeing you out of your hole.

375
00:29:34,532 --> 00:29:37,327
Egg drop challenge in
the team-building space.

376
00:29:38,244 --> 00:29:40,538
Tight department like us
gotta keep our synergy up.

377
00:29:40,622 --> 00:29:43,123
Egg drop challenge.
You buy this fucking shit?

378
00:29:43,207 --> 00:29:44,875
-And what are you guys doing?
-Felicia.

379
00:29:44,959 --> 00:29:48,128
Perpetuity wing visit.
Uh, this is Helly, our new refiner.

380
00:29:48,212 --> 00:29:49,562
-This is…
-Welcome, Helly.

381
00:29:50,590 --> 00:29:55,762
Well, we should get back.
Can't leave the nest empty.

382
00:29:56,804 --> 00:29:58,154
MARK: Good to see you, O&D.

383
00:30:00,266 --> 00:30:01,601
Those eggs look like shit.

384
00:30:07,690 --> 00:30:08,790
MARK: Irving, come on.

385
00:30:16,740 --> 00:30:17,741
Seth?

386
00:30:23,121 --> 00:30:24,873
[CHUCKLES] Natalie.

387
00:30:24,957 --> 00:30:26,959
Harmony, hello.

388
00:30:27,584 --> 00:30:30,796
I wasn't expecting…
[STAMMERS] Is this about Helena?

389
00:30:31,380 --> 00:30:33,506
No, this is about Peter Kilmer.

390
00:30:33,590 --> 00:30:36,134
And I should tell you that
the Board is joining us.

391
00:30:37,094 --> 00:30:38,512
[STATIC FEEDBACK ON SPEAKER]

392
00:30:48,522 --> 00:30:49,523
MS. COBEL: Hmm.

393
00:30:52,985 --> 00:30:54,486
[STATIC FEEDBACK CONTINUES]

394
00:31:03,704 --> 00:31:05,622
The Board would like you to speak first.

395
00:31:05,706 --> 00:31:07,207
Oh, yes. Of course.

396
00:31:07,791 --> 00:31:09,126
Salutations.

397
00:31:15,340 --> 00:31:17,759
So the search for Kilmer continues.

398
00:31:19,970 --> 00:31:21,888
[STATIC FEEDBACK CONTINUES]

399
00:31:24,516 --> 00:31:26,267
I will say this.

400
00:31:26,351 --> 00:31:29,521
Not to be alarmist, but
prior to his departure,

401
00:31:29,605 --> 00:31:34,484
there were some troubling
signs of possible… reintegration.

402
00:31:35,444 --> 00:31:38,155
[THE BOARD SPEAKING
ON EARPIECE, INDISTINCT]

403
00:31:39,656 --> 00:31:42,706
-[SPEAKING CONTINUES, INDISTINCT]
-[STATIC FEEDBACK CONTINUES]

404
00:31:47,998 --> 00:31:48,998
Okay. [CHUCKLES]

405
00:31:49,082 --> 00:31:52,126
So, the Board is conveying pretty strongly

406
00:31:52,210 --> 00:31:55,464
that the severance procedure
is provenly irreversible.

407
00:31:56,381 --> 00:31:57,382
Yes…

408
00:31:57,466 --> 00:32:01,344
And that this knowledge should be a given
for a person managing a severed floor.

409
00:32:01,428 --> 00:32:02,595
Yes, of course.

410
00:32:02,679 --> 00:32:05,682
While, of course, getting
MDR to their projected numbers

411
00:32:05,766 --> 00:32:07,934
by the quarterly deadline in three weeks.

412
00:32:08,018 --> 00:32:10,520
Yes, of course. [CHUCKLES]

413
00:32:10,604 --> 00:32:15,359
We are quickly rekindling our yield
down here with our nimble new refiner.

414
00:32:15,943 --> 00:32:19,493
-And for what it's worth, I'd like…
-The Board has concluded the call.

415
00:32:21,573 --> 00:32:24,159
Yes, okay. Thank you.

416
00:32:26,161 --> 00:32:28,372
-And may I ask…
-Goodbye, Harmony.

417
00:32:34,543 --> 00:32:36,461
HELLY: There's seriously
a two-person department?

418
00:32:36,545 --> 00:32:37,921
Like, they only ever see each other?

419
00:32:38,005 --> 00:32:41,216
For the most part. It's lonely, I suspect.

420
00:32:41,300 --> 00:32:43,302
And unnatural. Perverse.

421
00:32:43,386 --> 00:32:44,469
O&D is nice.

422
00:32:44,553 --> 00:32:47,306
No, they're not.
Nor do they share our values.

423
00:32:47,390 --> 00:32:49,349
Kier sorted the departments by virtue.

424
00:32:49,433 --> 00:32:53,145
Macrodats are clever and true,
while O&D's more cruelty-centered.

425
00:32:53,229 --> 00:32:54,354
How many departments are there?

426
00:32:54,438 --> 00:32:55,564
-Probably 30.
-Around five.

427
00:32:55,648 --> 00:32:57,024
No one's quite sure.

428
00:32:57,108 --> 00:32:59,985
O&D tried a violent coup
on the others decades ago,

429
00:33:00,069 --> 00:33:01,862
and that's why they
reduced them down to two.

430
00:33:01,946 --> 00:33:03,822
And that's why they keep
us all so far apart now.

431
00:33:03,906 --> 00:33:05,616
That's an absolute fiction.

432
00:33:05,700 --> 00:33:06,825
Did they ever kill anyone?

433
00:33:06,909 --> 00:33:09,536
Nope. There was no coup.
No one killed anyone.

434
00:33:09,620 --> 00:33:11,706
HELLY: Then why don't we ever hang out?

435
00:33:12,623 --> 00:33:14,959
I mean, I'm 99% sure there was no coup.

436
00:33:17,336 --> 00:33:20,130
If they come back and attack
us, I think we should kill Mark.

437
00:33:20,214 --> 00:33:21,340
-Oh, yeah?
-Yeah.

438
00:33:21,841 --> 00:33:23,800
So they think we're
crazy with nothing to lose.

439
00:33:23,884 --> 00:33:25,093
Smart. That's smart.

440
00:33:25,177 --> 00:33:28,180
Like, I'm imagining them rounding
a corner, and we're all blood-soaked,

441
00:33:28,264 --> 00:33:31,808
and I'm wearing your face, and
they're like, "Whose face is that?"

442
00:33:31,892 --> 00:33:35,146
And I'm like, "The last
person who fucked with us."

443
00:33:36,480 --> 00:33:38,857
And that's just feeling like a
really powerful image to me.

444
00:33:38,941 --> 00:33:42,361
Just… Seems like they'd recognize my face.

445
00:33:42,445 --> 00:33:44,045
Maybe if you wore it inside out?

446
00:33:53,956 --> 00:33:54,957
We're here.

447
00:34:10,765 --> 00:34:13,392
"Come now, children of my industry,

448
00:34:13,935 --> 00:34:16,853
and know the children of my blood."

449
00:34:19,690 --> 00:34:20,690
Whoa.

450
00:34:21,567 --> 00:34:27,823
That's Jame Eagan.
Current CEO. Remarkable man.

451
00:34:28,658 --> 00:34:31,619
Handsome too. See those brows?

452
00:34:54,808 --> 00:34:58,395
All the center displays
are for a past Eagan CEO.

453
00:34:59,145 --> 00:35:02,023
But the whole back part is Kier.

454
00:35:25,297 --> 00:35:28,382
KIER EAGAN: <i>I know
that death is near upon me,</i>

455
00:35:28,466 --> 00:35:34,681
<i>because people have begun to ask
what I see as my life's great achievement.</i>

456
00:35:35,265 --> 00:35:40,604
<i>They wish to know how they
should remember me as I rot.</i>

457
00:35:41,354 --> 00:35:48,194
<i>In my life, I have identified four
components, which I call tempers,</i>

458
00:35:48,278 --> 00:35:52,324
<i>from which are derived every human soul.</i>

459
00:35:52,907 --> 00:35:58,330
<i>Woe. Frolic. Dread. Malice.</i>

460
00:35:59,205 --> 00:36:04,877
<i>Each man's character is
defined by the precise ratio</i>

461
00:36:04,961 --> 00:36:06,713
<i>that resides in him.</i>

462
00:36:07,464 --> 00:36:12,969
<i>I walked into the cave of my
own mind, and there I tamed them.</i>

463
00:36:13,053 --> 00:36:17,598
<i>Should you tame the tempers as I did mine,</i>

464
00:36:17,682 --> 00:36:21,394
<i>then the world shall
become but your appendage.</i>

465
00:36:21,478 --> 00:36:25,189
<i>It is this great and consecrated power</i>

466
00:36:25,273 --> 00:36:30,570
<i>that I hope to pass on
to all of you, my children.</i>

467
00:36:32,781 --> 00:36:34,949
MYRTLE EAGAN: <i>I think that to be an Eagan,</i>

468
00:36:35,033 --> 00:36:39,871
<i>either a true Eagan or anyone
working in this Lumon family,</i>

469
00:36:39,955 --> 00:36:42,832
<i>what you are is the keeper of an ethos,</i>

470
00:36:42,916 --> 00:36:47,670
<i>a compact of values that we
have long held as precious,</i>

471
00:36:47,754 --> 00:36:51,883
<i>and which I do believe
will one day save this world.</i>

472
00:36:51,967 --> 00:36:54,385
<i>And that ethos goes all the way back</i>

473
00:36:54,469 --> 00:36:56,179
-<i>through my blood…</i>
-Oh, look, you got one.

474
00:36:56,263 --> 00:36:58,556
<i>…to where we all started, with Kier.</i>

475
00:36:58,640 --> 00:37:01,140
-It's okay if you cry.
-MYRTLE: <i>When I was a girl,</i>

476
00:37:01,601 --> 00:37:03,227
<i>my father would make me whisper…</i>

477
00:37:03,311 --> 00:37:06,011
-She was five CEOs ago.
-[MYRTLE CONTINUES, INDISTINCT]

478
00:37:07,190 --> 00:37:08,817
They'd never had a woman before.

479
00:37:09,567 --> 00:37:14,447
And she told her father at age
seven, she was going to be the first.

480
00:37:14,531 --> 00:37:16,157
-[MARK, IRVING CHUCKLE]
-MYRTLE: <i>Vision. Verve.</i>

481
00:37:16,241 --> 00:37:17,783
-Isn't that lovely?
-<i>Wit.</i>

482
00:37:17,867 --> 00:37:19,119
-It's beautiful.
-<i>Cheer.</i>

483
00:37:19,744 --> 00:37:22,996
It almost makes me wish I
remembered my own childhood.

484
00:37:23,080 --> 00:37:27,876
MYRTLE: <i>Nimbleness. Probity. Wiles.</i>

485
00:37:28,711 --> 00:37:34,049
It's an unnatural state for
a person to have no history.

486
00:37:34,550 --> 00:37:40,097
History makes us someone.
Gives us a context. A shape.

487
00:37:40,764 --> 00:37:45,019
But waking up on that
table, I was shapeless.

488
00:37:47,104 --> 00:37:52,275
But then I learned that
I work for a company

489
00:37:52,359 --> 00:37:58,365
that has been actively
caring for mankind since 1866.

490
00:37:59,116 --> 00:38:00,200
Look.

491
00:38:00,284 --> 00:38:05,623
Each of these is a real smile
from someone on the outside.

492
00:38:06,332 --> 00:38:09,501
Someone Lumon Industries has helped.

493
00:38:10,169 --> 00:38:12,504
And they rotate these through.

494
00:38:12,588 --> 00:38:16,884
The true number of smiles
may well be in the millions.

495
00:38:18,636 --> 00:38:20,428
So what are we, like, a dental company?

496
00:38:20,512 --> 00:38:21,638
[CHUCKLING] No.

497
00:38:21,722 --> 00:38:24,724
My point is you're part of a history now.

498
00:38:24,808 --> 00:38:27,352
-A noble one.
-And that's so much more mea…

499
00:38:27,436 --> 00:38:28,786
They update the mouth wall?

500
00:38:29,021 --> 00:38:31,731
It's not called the "mouth wall."

501
00:38:31,815 --> 00:38:33,733
Damn, they took down the chick I like.

502
00:38:33,817 --> 00:38:35,277
[INHALES SHARPLY]
Are you guys ready to go?

503
00:38:35,361 --> 00:38:36,611
IRVING: We just got here.

504
00:38:38,280 --> 00:38:40,741
She hasn't even seen the Kier part yet.

505
00:38:44,954 --> 00:38:45,955
Gerhardt.

506
00:39:00,177 --> 00:39:02,805
-Jesus.
-No. Kier.

507
00:39:04,014 --> 00:39:05,056
It's his house?

508
00:39:05,140 --> 00:39:07,142
A perfect replica.

509
00:39:07,226 --> 00:39:08,227
Pretty cool, huh?

510
00:39:09,353 --> 00:39:11,355
Yeah, pretty cool.

511
00:39:40,217 --> 00:39:45,306
The bedroom of Kier,
just as he might have left it.

512
00:39:49,684 --> 00:39:51,060
Well, this is thorough.

513
00:39:52,061 --> 00:39:55,773
I hate this place.
It smells like 19th century ass.

514
00:39:55,857 --> 00:39:59,360
Stop treating this like
some kind of beer party.

515
00:39:59,444 --> 00:40:02,244
That's not fair.
Up till now, I've been reverent as shit.

516
00:40:03,573 --> 00:40:04,623
-IRVING: Mark.
-Yeah?

517
00:40:07,035 --> 00:40:10,829
Were you about to… bed sit?

518
00:40:10,913 --> 00:40:13,583
No. Hmm? Nuh-uh.

519
00:40:15,960 --> 00:40:19,296
What's that in your, uh, pocket, Mark?
Hmm?

520
00:40:19,380 --> 00:40:22,591
Oh, um… I, um…

521
00:40:22,675 --> 00:40:26,888
Oh, God, Mark. Still, hmm?

522
00:40:27,931 --> 00:40:29,765
It's… for fun.

523
00:40:29,849 --> 00:40:31,351
It's the perpetuity wing.

524
00:40:32,060 --> 00:40:33,769
It's the Eagans.

525
00:40:33,853 --> 00:40:38,190
It's the living soul of Lumon
and everything she stands for,

526
00:40:38,274 --> 00:40:39,650
not a bingo match.

527
00:40:39,734 --> 00:40:43,362
Look, Kier said, "Keep a
merry humor ever in your heart."

528
00:40:43,446 --> 00:40:46,240
[CHUCKLES] Oh.
Did you give one to the trainee? Hmm?

529
00:40:47,533 --> 00:40:48,783
-Of course, you did.
-I…

530
00:40:49,953 --> 00:40:52,121
She's starved for meaning here.

531
00:40:52,205 --> 00:40:54,790
So, of course, you'd pervert
our founder's message

532
00:40:54,874 --> 00:40:57,293
with jokes and impiety.

533
00:40:57,377 --> 00:41:01,338
That's definitely what she
needs from her department chief.

534
00:41:01,422 --> 00:41:03,257
Hey, I didn't want to be department chief.

535
00:41:03,341 --> 00:41:06,010
I didn't ask for Petey
to disappear and for…

536
00:41:10,974 --> 00:41:11,975
Helly?

537
00:41:13,810 --> 00:41:14,811
Uh-oh.

538
00:41:15,603 --> 00:41:16,604
Helly?

539
00:41:17,355 --> 00:41:18,940
Shit.

540
00:41:47,176 --> 00:41:48,177
Helly!

541
00:41:50,305 --> 00:41:54,851
[CHUCKLES] This is… Jesus.

542
00:42:15,954 --> 00:42:16,955
Helly!

543
00:42:22,210 --> 00:42:24,630
[ALARM WAILING]

544
00:42:30,260 --> 00:42:31,470
Helly!

545
00:42:36,725 --> 00:42:37,726
MARK: Helly!

546
00:42:40,520 --> 00:42:42,564
No! [GROANING, STRAINING]

547
00:42:44,024 --> 00:42:45,901
MARK: Goddamn it, Helly.

548
00:42:53,075 --> 00:42:54,076
[ALARM STOPS]

549
00:42:57,996 --> 00:42:59,748
This way, please, Helly R.

550
00:43:00,624 --> 00:43:01,625
[HELLY PANTING]

551
00:43:06,588 --> 00:43:08,090
[BREATHING SHAKILY]

552
00:43:34,992 --> 00:43:35,993
[SCANNER BEEPS]

553
00:43:39,788 --> 00:43:40,789
On you go.

554
00:44:15,032 --> 00:44:17,034
I'm truly sorry to see you here, Helly.

555
00:44:25,959 --> 00:44:27,836
[HELLY SUCKS TEETH, SHUDDERS]

556
00:44:27,920 --> 00:44:30,020
MR. MILCHICK: I
hoped you'd just settle in.

557
00:44:32,174 --> 00:44:35,427
I know Mark's been trying so
hard to get you to feel happy.

558
00:44:44,018 --> 00:44:48,856
Look, you seem like a smart person.
But don't you see how fucked up this is?

559
00:44:49,357 --> 00:44:51,357
-You can't keep…
-Not right now, Helly.

560
00:44:53,194 --> 00:44:55,321
Sit. Please.

561
00:45:11,629 --> 00:45:13,172
Hands on the table, please.

562
00:45:52,545 --> 00:45:55,195
I'm bringing up the compunction
statement for Helly R.

563
00:45:59,302 --> 00:46:00,428
[GASPS] Wha…

564
00:46:01,637 --> 00:46:02,638
What is this?

565
00:46:06,309 --> 00:46:07,310
Read it.

566
00:46:10,771 --> 00:46:11,772
I don't want to.

567
00:46:13,357 --> 00:46:15,359
No. Do.

568
00:46:18,946 --> 00:46:19,947
[SCOFFS]

569
00:46:21,907 --> 00:46:25,453
"Forgive me for the harm
I have caused this world.

570
00:46:26,495 --> 00:46:29,915
"None may atone for my actions but me,"

571
00:46:29,999 --> 00:46:32,710
"and only in me shall
their stain live on."

572
00:46:33,920 --> 00:46:36,588
"I am thankful to have been caught,"

573
00:46:36,672 --> 00:46:39,050
"my fall cut short by
those with wizened hands."

574
00:46:39,717 --> 00:46:41,917
All I can be is sorry,
and that is all I am."

575
00:46:45,973 --> 00:46:47,475
I'm afraid you don't mean it.

576
00:46:49,435 --> 00:46:50,436
Excuse me?

577
00:46:52,146 --> 00:46:53,546
MR. MILCHICK: Again, please.

578
00:46:56,108 --> 00:46:58,778
"Forgive me for the harm
I have caused this world.

579
00:47:00,196 --> 00:47:03,073
"None may atone for my actions but me,"

580
00:47:04,115 --> 00:47:07,410
"and only in me shall
their stain live on."

581
00:47:07,994 --> 00:47:10,038
"I am thankful to have been caught,"

582
00:47:10,622 --> 00:47:13,291
"my fall cut short by
those with wizened hands."

583
00:47:14,125 --> 00:47:16,962
All I can be is sorry,
and that is all I am."

584
00:47:23,343 --> 00:47:24,511
Again.

585
00:47:27,264 --> 00:47:28,265
Really?

586
00:47:33,770 --> 00:47:34,771
DYLAN: Fun day.

587
00:47:36,731 --> 00:47:37,732
See you tomorrow.

588
00:47:39,734 --> 00:47:40,735
Yeah.

589
00:47:42,445 --> 00:47:43,529
Do you want me to get these?

590
00:47:43,613 --> 00:47:45,991
MARK: [SIGHS] Yeah, go ahead.

591
00:47:58,336 --> 00:47:59,337
[SIGHS]

592
00:48:01,089 --> 00:48:02,090
[GRUNTS]

593
00:49:35,057 --> 00:49:36,558
[PANTING]

594
00:49:46,026 --> 00:49:48,028
[PANTING]

595
00:49:57,413 --> 00:49:59,915
[GRUNTS, BREATHING HEAVILY]

596
00:50:02,251 --> 00:50:06,046
I haven't any tokens.
I can't eat with no tokens.

597
00:50:13,721 --> 00:50:14,722
Hey!

598
00:50:15,806 --> 00:50:17,516
I need tokens so I can eat!

599
00:50:18,934 --> 00:50:20,185
[GRUNTS]

600
00:50:22,396 --> 00:50:23,439
[CHOKES]

601
00:50:27,234 --> 00:50:28,109
CASHIER: Oh, my God.

602
00:50:28,193 --> 00:50:29,243
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

603
00:50:30,404 --> 00:50:31,279
[SHOUTS]

604
00:50:31,363 --> 00:50:33,407
[HIGH-PITCHED RINGING]

605
00:50:36,744 --> 00:50:37,870
[ELEVATOR BELL DINGS]

606
00:51:34,468 --> 00:51:35,469
MARK: Petey?

607
00:51:41,684 --> 00:51:42,685
Petey?

608
00:52:06,332 --> 00:52:08,334
[SIREN WAILING]

609
00:52:28,563 --> 00:52:30,064
[WAILING CONTINUES]

610
00:52:59,302 --> 00:53:00,803
[POLICE RADIO CHATTER]

611
00:53:04,390 --> 00:53:05,933
[CASHIER SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

612
00:53:06,017 --> 00:53:07,434
EMT 1: All right now. Take it slow.

613
00:53:07,518 --> 00:53:09,854
[SPEAKING CONTINUES, INDISTINCT]

614
00:53:09,938 --> 00:53:11,689
[EMT 1 CHATTERING, INDISTINCT]

615
00:53:11,773 --> 00:53:13,732
Did you happen to see
which direction he came from?

616
00:53:13,816 --> 00:53:15,734
[CASHIER SPEAKS, INDISTINCT]

617
00:53:15,818 --> 00:53:18,320
I'm just gonna grab the stretcher.
I'll be right back. [INDISTINCT]

618
00:53:18,404 --> 00:53:20,454
OFFICER 1: Can you get an ID on this guy?

619
00:53:20,865 --> 00:53:23,242
[OFFICERS, EMTS CHATTERING, INDISTINCT]

620
00:53:23,326 --> 00:53:26,620
OFFICER 1: I guess he's from nearby.
He came in with a robe on. That was it.

621
00:53:26,704 --> 00:53:28,455
[EMT 2 SPEAKING, INDISTINCT]

622
00:53:28,539 --> 00:53:30,041
[BREATHES SHAKILY]

623
00:53:34,796 --> 00:53:36,630
[BREATHES SHAKILY]

624
00:53:36,714 --> 00:53:37,673
[GROANS]

625
00:53:37,757 --> 00:53:39,383
EMT 1: Whoa, whoa, whoa.
He's down. He's down.

626
00:53:39,467 --> 00:53:42,094
-EMT 2: Whoa, whoa, whoa.
-Sir. Sir, sir, sir. Can you hear me?

627
00:53:42,178 --> 00:53:43,762
OFFICER 1: You got that? Just stand back.

628
00:53:43,846 --> 00:53:48,642
OFFICER 2: Yeah, dispatch, I got a
10-53 at a convenience store on 42nd.

629
00:53:48,726 --> 00:53:49,978
Call that in, please.

630
00:53:51,604 --> 00:53:53,772
-EMT 1: Is he breathing?
-EMT 2: No, he's not breathing, man.

631
00:53:53,856 --> 00:53:55,556
Yeah, yeah, yeah. Give me the bpm.

632
00:53:56,901 --> 00:54:00,001
-OFFICER 2: You okay? You okay?
-EMT 1: Get him on the board.

633
00:54:11,249 --> 00:54:13,459
[POLICE RADIO CHATTER]

634
00:55:07,429 --> 00:55:09,181
[PANTING]

635
00:55:15,187 --> 00:55:17,606
-[SIGHS]
-[BUZZING]

636
00:55:25,030 --> 00:55:26,531
[BUZZING CONTINUES]

